WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
we all know the name rosa parks whose  defiance on a montgomery alabama bus  became a turning point in the civil  rights movement back in 1955 but chances  are you may not have heard about this  woman her name is claudette colvin most  people don't know her story as a  teenager she was arrested nine months  before parks for the very same reason  refusing to give up her busy to a white  person her story is a very important  part of our civil rights history and now  there's a new development that you'll  see only on cbs mornings and we love  when this happens lead national  correspondent that's you david beck now  has an interview that we will and it's  got a twist as only you can do i've been  waiting for this all morning i can't  wait for you to see this and all of you  you don't hear about claudette colvin in  the history books but she should she's  now a great grandmother and for the past  66 years she has lived with a criminal  record  in what may be her final protest she  asked a judge in alabama to clear her  name by expunging her record  and the judge did it this is her story  and as gail said you do not want to miss  the surprise at the end  needing assistance now it's very nice to  meet you claudette colvin has spent most  of her 82 years as a convicted juvenile  delinquent who would go on to college  work as a nurse's aide and raise two  sons do you think of yourself as an  important figure in history no i think  of myself  as a survivor of the civil rights  struggle her place in the civil rights  struggle began when she was 15 years old  at the back of a crowded montgomery  alabama bus after school with three  friends i was sitting in the  session that was allowed for  a couple of people  and this white lady came the bus driver  demanded for the four seats and this is  you and three others students yes so  three of them got up yes and you refused  so i refused  because this wasn't an elderly white  lady this was a young white lady you're  sitting there  your classmates get up and move  yes what was going through your head as  to why you refused to stand  i said  i could not move because history had me  glued to the feet  and they said how is that i said well it  felt as though  harriet tubman hands were pushing me  down on one shoulder and sojourner's  truth hand  was pushing me down on the other  shoulder  now that happened nine months before  rosa parks was arrested for refusing to  give up her seat on a montgomery bus  overshadowing for decades what a  teenager had also done and the traffic  patroller came to the rear of the bus  and asked me  why i wasn't sitting there i told him i  was sitting there because  i paid my fare and it's my  constitutional rights  now watching this interview from a  nearby room was judge calvin williams  today he is the presiding judge of the  montgomery family court and then what  happened oh lo and behold two  squad car patrolman stopped and you know  cut the bus off and  came they stopped and we said oh man  we invited the judge to listen in  and hear colvin's account of what the  police did to her in 1955.  and he asked me why was i'm sitting  there  and i was even more defiant and i said i  paid my affair and it's my  constitutional rights he said  constitutional rights and i don't know  and they put me in the squad car  and their hand caught me through the  window claudette colvin was jailed  charged with disturbing the peace  breaking segregation law and found  guilty of assaulting police officers  clawing them with her fingernails they  said i clawed  the policemen and i kicked the police i  didn't do all of that was it the one and  only time in your life that you've gone  to jail yes  at the time i represented claudette  carvin there was no rosa parks case  as a young attorney fred gray later  portrayed by cuba gooding jr in the film  selma took up her case that's before he  went on to defend rosa parks in his book  he pays tribute the whole history of the  civil rights movement  may have been different  but  for claudette at the age of 16 she made  headlines again as one of four  plaintiffs in the lawsuit that would end  alabama's bus segregation  in the landmark browder versus gale  supreme court decision  when you won in court yes you could sit  in the front row if you want yes  and did you  yes  but the charge of assaulting police  officers remained fast forward to today  when judge calvin williams in montgomery  alabama read her appeal he expunged her  66 year old criminal record and i'm no  longer a juvenile delinquent at 82. she  was not present when the judge ruled she  didn't know his name she didn't even  know what he looked like we have a  surprise  it was a surprise we have a surprise for  you what is this advice  someone would like to meet you  what's up  hello miss calvin  judge calvin williams  church calvin williams oh my goodness  lord  [Laughter]  oh my goodness on behalf of myself  and all of the judges of montgomery  offer my apology  for an injustice  that was perpetrated upon you wow this  is a surprise  [Laughter]  so you didn't know that the judge who  did this was african-american no  and i'm so proud  the judge wasn't even born when miss  colvin refused to give up her seat on  that bus when she did this in 1955 there  were no african-american judges in  montgomery  she stood up for right  and now i'm the beneficiary and  byproduct of that and i could correct  the wrong  that was done to her it meant to that  amen to that can i squeeze your hand yes  ma'am please do  so great  oh  david she's giggling like a little girl  all morning when we said surprise you  won't want to miss something and i hope  it lives up to it but it did it did and  i love tissues here at the table yes i  love how she called on the ancestors  harriet and sojourner who had her hands  on had their hands on her shoulders and  said don't get up i'm glued to the seat  because of history  beautifully done she had been studying  black history in school and it became so  important to her that she just felt  moved that's right  before the movement itself with rosa  parks had taken off she was independent  and you may be wondering why why not  claudette colvin why rosa parks why is  that the one we hear about yes and  according to miss colvin she was 15 and  pregnant  and so she said the civil rights leaders  didn't want a young rebel rouser  pregnant girl to represent the movement  mrs parks was a well-respected secretary  of the naacp who according to miss  colvin  was lighter in color and they thought  that it would be more appealing to put  her forward as a face of the movie and  those were the times we were living  in her graciousness says but that's not  to take anything away from what miss  parker of course right  um  but and and why now you may be wondering  well like why why do now you're 82. she  said when i saw the george floyd  protests  it kind of reignited a spirit in her an  activist spirit and she said i just  wanted my kids and the great-grandbabies  to see that even at 82 in a wheelchair  yeah i still had a fight left you know  and we saw kids her age and younger out  there she was 15. david that's right 15  it's a reminder we are walking in her  footsteps yes we are thank you claudette  colvin great job what a lady the  surprise lives up to it well done david  thank you very much

